---
# FLUX, WATCHED BECAUSE IT IS NOW THE DEPLOY PATH.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THESE RULES ARE ACTUALLY FOR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A deploy used to be a CI job, and a broken deploy was a red job in a list somebody reads. It is
# now four controllers agreeing: source-controller fetches the commit, image-reflector-controller
# scans the registry, image-automation-controller commits the winning tag, kustomize-controller
# applies it. Any of them can stop WITHOUT ANYTHING GOING RED, because a custom resource turning
# `Ready=False` is not an event that reaches a human.
#
# The failure that produces is not an outage. It is the cluster serving the last image it happened
# to receive, forever, while every CI run stays green -- the same silent staleness that caused the
# 2026-09-03 outage and put Flux here to begin with. Replacing a loud failure with a quiet one
# would have been a downgrade, so these rules exist as a condition of retiring `kubectl set image`
# rather than as a follow-up to it.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE NO LONGER WATCHES WHETHER A RECONCILE SUCCEEDS. It was written around
# `gotk_reconcile_condition`, whose 0/1-per-(kind,name,type,status) shape is why the rules said
# `== 1` rather than testing for the series -- an expression that omits the comparison fires the
# moment Flux reports anything at all. That care was correct and is now moot: this fleet's Flux has
# never published that gauge, nor `gotk_suspend_status`, so the three rules built on them were
# permanently green. They are gone, replaced by one that watches reconciles CEASING, which is what
# the remaining metrics can actually say. Reconciliation FAILURE and a SUSPENDED object are
# unwatched here until kube-state-metrics is given a `--custom-resource-state-config` for Flux's
# custom resources, and that config lives in the gitops repository. See the note on the rule.
groups:
  - name: flux
    rules:
      # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THREE ALERTS STOOD HERE THAT COULD NEVER FIRE, AND THAT IS WORTH RECORDING RATHER THAN
      # QUIETLY REPLACING
      # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      #
      # `FluxReconciliationFailing`, `FluxDeployPathStalled` and `FluxSuspended` selected on
      # `gotk_reconcile_condition` and `gotk_suspend_status`. Neither has EVER had a series on this
      # fleet -- `count(max_over_time(...[30d]))` returns 0 for both, against 22 for the duration
      # counter beside them. Newer flux controllers dropped those gauges; the only `gotk_*` families
      # published here are `gotk_reconcile_duration_seconds_{bucket,count,sum}` and `gotk_token_*`.
      # So all three were permanently green for the same reason a deleted alert is green, and the
      # deploy path they guarded was unwatched for as long as they existed.
      #
      # ⚠️ "NO SERIES RIGHT NOW" IS NOT THE TEST, and using it as one produces false accusations.
      # `kube_pod_container_status_waiting_reason` and `nixos_auto_apply_blocked_reason` both read
      # zero at an arbitrary instant and both are healthy: they are published only while a pod is
      # waiting or a host is blocked, so absence is the good state. The question is whether a metric
      # has EVER had a series, over a window longer than the condition it reports. Asking it the
      # other way nearly cost K3sPodCrashLooping, which is fine, a needless rewrite.
      #
      # WHAT REPLACES THEM IS WEAKER, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. A stopped reconciler and a FAILING
      # one are different failures, and only the first is detectable from what Flux emits here: the
      # duration histogram counts reconciles, so it can say they have ceased, but not that one came
      # back NotReady. A Kustomization that reconciles briskly and fails every time still increments
      # this counter and stays silent here.
      #
      # RESTORING THE FAILURE AND SUSPEND SIGNALS needs kube-state-metrics to expose Flux's custom
      # resources through `--custom-resource-state-config`, which lives in the gitops repository
      # rather than in this one. Until that exists, this file does NOT watch reconciliation failure
      # or a suspended object, and the absence of an alert here is not evidence that Flux is
      # applying cleanly.
      #
      # ONE HOUR AGAINST A MEASURED CADENCE, not a round number: the busiest objects reconcile 12-14
      # times an hour (ImageUpdateAutomation/movies 14.1, GitRepository/movies 12.1), so an hour at
      # zero is far outside normal for anything that reconciles at all. Per-series, so it names the
      # object that stopped rather than the fleet.
      - alert: FluxReconciliationStopped
        expr: increase(gotk_reconcile_duration_seconds_count[1h]) == 0
        # The `increase` window is already an hour; this only confirms it is not one odd sample at a
        # scrape boundary.
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.kind }}/{{ $labels.name }} has not reconciled for an hour"
          description: >-
            `gotk_reconcile_duration_seconds_count` for this object has not moved in an hour, while
            the busiest objects on this cluster reconcile 12-14 times an hour. Either its controller
            is wedged, the object is suspended, or its source is unreachable. This is the DEPLOY
            PATH: a stopped Kustomization means merged changes stop reaching pods, silently, with CI
            green and the image built -- the state that hid a read-model fix for hours on
            2026-09-07. Check `flux get all -A` on monitoring-1 and the controller's own output in
            the log store (`namespace:flux-system`). This alert does NOT watch reconciliation
            FAILURE: an object that reconciles and fails every time keeps this counter moving and
            stays silent here.


      # THE EXPORTER ITSELF. Without this the three rules above go quiet exactly when the thing
      # they watch dies hardest, because an absent series cannot compare `== 1`.
      - alert: FluxControllerDown
        expr: up{job="flux"} == 0
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.controller }} is not answering its metrics port"
          description: >-
            Either the controller is down or its NodePort no longer matches. The ports are fixed in
            movies-gitops/flux-metrics/services.yaml and must agree with
            `fleet.prometheus.fluxTargets`; nothing checks that, and a disagreement looks exactly
            like this. Check `kubectl -n flux-system get pods` first -- if they are Running, it is
            the port.
